From: freeman@netcom.com (Jay Reynolds Freeman)
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 1994 09:24:40 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-300] BABY: Re: The Procreation Instinct

    I have had at least one good female friend report strong "baby
lust".  She was in her early 30s at the time, and already had one
child, then not quite ten.  But she did not have another child, though
she was living with someone to whom she subsequently became married,
and thus had plenty of opportunity.
    And, offhand, I can think of two other women I know who have had
operations for permanent sterility (insert canonical comment about
meaninglessness of "permanent" in the context of developing medical
technology etc. etc.) and seem quite happy with them.  Both are late
30-ish.  One had the operation in her teens, one a few years ago.
Neither has children.

    In a lower-division cultural anthropology class I took at San Jose
State University in spring 1994, we studied reproductive practices in
many cultures.  It appeared that most cultures, from hunter-gatherer
on up, have regularly used practices that result in quite effective
control of reproductive rate.  (I can't call them all "birth control",
since one of the common ones is killing young children, either overtly
and knowingly or by neglect.)  Such cultures do not always bring their
net reproductive rates to zero, but there are often clear notions of
what constitutes "too many" or "too frequent" children, which the
means of control avoid.  Thus for example, in hunter-gatherer bands it
may be very undesirable to conceive child N+1 before child N is old
enough not to need to be carried in arms as the band moves about.  I
was left with rather the impression that the modern west is an outlier
in human societies, in so sparingly applying the birth control
mechanisms it does have.
    Another point from the class was that in many societies there was
a strong correlation between the desirability of children and their
economic benefit to parents.  Thus in some cultures that grow food via
labor-intensive irrigated agriculture -- think rice paddies -- a child
of eight produces more than enough food to compensate for his or her
drain on food and other family resources.  That's because planting,
transplanting, and harvesting rice require persistant dexterity rather
than physical strength.  In such cultures, families want lots of
children, for greater economic wealth, and don't care which sex.  In
other cultures, where for one reason or another one sex of child is
more desirable, child-care practices often favor the desired sex, so
that the other sex has a higher death rate in childhood.  And so on.

    Was it Nick Szabo who talked about the "self-genocide" phenomenon?
Perhaps he could re-mail me his citations; I missed that posting and
it is rather in contradiction to what we were taught in this course.

    I sense a possible need to split hairs carefully in the equating
of "seeking population control" with "entropic death worship" (or
whatever).  If an individual believed in personal boundless expansion
and increasing consumption of resources, it might be entirely rational
and non-entropic for that individual to seek restricted population
growth in the surrounding culture.  I suggest that it is necessary to
distinguish between entropic effects of population control on specific
individuals already living and such effects on their culture.

                                               --  Jay Freeman


