From: EdRegis@aol.com
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 1994 12:55:09 -0400
Subject: [#94-10-301] Argument by Analogy (Was: Reply to Challenged Uploaders)

Derek Zahn writes (Oct 16, 1994 3:06 PM):
>Your simply knowing it when you see it 
>makes Ed Regis the judge of consciousness...

Not exactly: it makes *any* self-conscious being the judge of consciousness.
 

>Am I conscious (I did meet you briefly at Extro 1, 
>so you have good reason to think that I'm not an AI program)?  >Assuming
'yes', how do you arrive at that conclusion?  

Atypically, I think that academic philosophy actually helps here.  In the
history of philosophy this subject comes under the heading of the "Other
Minds" problem: How do we know there are other minds?  The classical answer
is: by analogy.  The argument goes: (1) I know that I am conscious by
personal experience.  (2) Other organisms that are substantially similar to
me--in terms of behavior, physical, physiological, biological, brain, neural,
and molecular-biological structure, etc., et al.--are probably substantially
similar in other ways too, including their possessing consciousness.
 Therefore (conclusion), other people possess consciousness.  (The Argument
from Analogy.)

The classical way of attacking the argument is by saying that no argument
from analogy is ever perfect, which is true.  (If there were a perfect
similarity the relationship between me and others would not be one of
analogy, it would be an identity.)  There are answers to that attack, viz.:
what the argument proves is that their conscious experience is not identical
with mine, but only similar, which to me is a good answer.  (There is a whole
industry, in academic philosophy, which discusses this argument and replies
to it.)  

Recent experiments in neurophysiology add some force to the argument that it
didn't have in earlier times.  These experiments show that some conscious
states can be correlated to electrical activity in the brain; that changes in
consciousness states can be produced by applied changes in the brain's
physical states, etc.  You get the idea.  (Call this the Sub-Argument from
Neurophysiology.)  

So, if you ask me how I know that you and other people are conscious, my
answer is: by the Argument from Analogy, with the extra-added attraction, the
Sub-Argument from Neurophysiology.

>Is a dog conscious?

Very probably, on the basis of both the arguments described above.  The
analogy between dogs and humans is still substantially similar, although less
so than between humans, and the same types of neurophysiological experments
have been performed on dogs.  What the Argument from Analogy shows here is
that the dog's conscious experience is probably more different from my own
than is that of another person.  

>If the answer to the above question is 'yes', 
>is a bacterium conscious?

You've got me there, but on the basis of the Argument from Analogy, the
answer is probably no: the analogy between humans and bacteria is too weak
for the conclusion to be drawn that bacteria are conscious.  

